for drug-induced hypercalcemia, such as milk-alkali syndrome?", "summary": "Calcium-based antacid overuse and low urine sodium are characteristic of milk-alkali syndrome. This provides moderate support for B3 and weakens malignancy if drug-induced hypercalcemia is confirmed."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of a new-onset cough without granulomatous disease evidence argue for sarcoidosis as a reactive/inflammatory hypercalcemia cause?", "summary": "While sarcoidosis can cause hypercalcemia with cough, absence of granulomatous evidence (ACE, biopsy) and the subacute presentation makes this less likely. Weakens B4 but has limited impact on other branches."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the low 24-hour urine sodium (5 mmol) argue against malignancy with hypercalcemia and favor a drug-induced cause?", "summary": "Low urine sodium is atypical for malignancy-associated hypercalcemia but common in milk-alkali syndrome. This weakens B1 and strengthens B3 in the differential."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the lack of parathyroid hormone elevation in the evidence argue against endocrine/metabolic hypercalcemia in favor of malignancy with hypercalcemia?", "summary": "Absence of PTH elevation removes support for primary hyperparathyroidism, a key endocrine cause. This strengthens malignancy (B1) as the most likely explanation."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the patient's antacid formulation calcium-based, which would support drug-induced hypercalcemia, or does it contain non-calcium agents, arguing against this diagnosis?", "summary": "Calcium-based antacid use is a direct cause of milk-alkali syndrome. Without this information, the evidence remains neutral, but potential support for B3 exists if confirmed."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of granulomatous disease confirmation (e.g., no elevated ACE, no biopsy) argue against reactive/inflammatory hypercalcemia as the etiology?", "summary": "Without granulomatous disease evidence, sarcoidosis and other inflammatory causes are unlikely. This provides moderate against for B4."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.681], ["B1.1", 0.4], ["B3", 0.28]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No immediate life-threatening instability markers (airway, respiratory, hemodynamic) or time-critical syndrome patterns (e.g., STEMI, septic shock) are present. Hypercalcemia (12.5 mg/dL) is subacute with no acute neurological deterioration, renal failure, or cardiac instability requiring urgent intervention.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading hypothesis (B1.1: Humoral Hypercalcemia of Malignancy) has posterior=0.4 < 0.75. Dangerous alternative B1 (posterior=0.68, danger=0.9) remains unresolved. Frontier contains unexplored branches requiring further diagnostic clarification."}, "turn_budget_used": 1, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.7, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.40047380052660164, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely explanation of this patient's current presentation?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Antacid overuse"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Increased 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Increased parathyroid hormone"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Malignancy"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Viral illness"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Granulomatosis'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "program_faults": [], "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.681}, "leader": {"branch_id