de:\n[RAG context for 'A 45-year-old African American woman presents to her primary care physician for not feeling well' (score=0.65):]\n  Source: Hypercalcemia of Malignancy. > Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes\n  Malignancy is the most common cause of hypercalcemia in hospitalized patients. Although most cases are managed on an outpatient basis, severe hypercalcemia often requires inpatient treatment. The prog\n[RAG context for 'She states she has had a cough for the past week' (score=0.70):]\n  Source: InternalMed_Harrison\n  Ordinarily, the diagnosis of cancer hypercalcemia is not difficult because tumor symptoms are prominent when hypercalcemia is detected. Indeed, hypercalcemia may be noted incidentally during the worku\n[RAG context for 'She states she has had a cough for the past week' (score=0.70):]\n  Source: Hypercalcemia of Malignancy. > Pearls and Other Issues\n  Key facts to keep in mind regarding malignancy-related hypercalcemia include:\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  takes antacids for poorly controlled GERD → milk-alkali syndrome → Milk-Alkali Syndrome from Calcium-Based Antacid Overuse\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Assess whether the patient's antacid use (especially calcium-based) and symptoms (abdominal pain, hypercalcemia) support a diagnosis of milk-alkali syndrome\n  cough with recent weight loss → paraneoplastic hypercalcemia → Humoral Hypercalcemia of Malignancy (Paraneoplastic)\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate for underlying malignancy (e.g., lung cancer, lymphoma) that could cause paraneoplastic hypercalcemia with associated constitutional symptoms\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
